PUBLIC COMMENTS (3 Minute Time Limit) - 15 speakers {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mike Van Voorhis, President of the Sunset Beach Community Association, was called to speak and invited everyone to the Sunset Beach Halloween Party on Saturday, October 27, 6-10 pm, at the Community Center on 12th Street and adjoining green belt.  This year's theme is Super Heroes and there are activities for the whole family.  \par
\par
Assemblyman Matthew Harper was called to speak and stated his support for Consent Calendar Item 19 - 18-401 C) regarding examining the State's property tax apportionment system, and offered his assistance if approved.\par
\par
Alan Gandall, American Youth Soccer Organization (AYSO) Area K Director, was called to speak and provided a report on AYSO in Huntington Beach, which works with some 6,000 youth.\par
\par
Joseph Riedel, victim of a violent crime in Huntington Beach, was called to speak and stated his disappointment at the lack of response from the Huntington Beach Police Department in an incident in which he received major injuries.  \par
\par
Stephen Knight, resident of Huntington Beach, was called to speak and shared his opinions on sober living family units vs detox and residential treatment centers, including the State agencies which provide operating rules and regulations.   \par
\par
Naui H., who described himself as an Orange County activist, was called to speak and shared his opinions about people at various rallies across the country, particularly Gracey Van Der Mark.\par
\par
Diane Lee was called to speak and stated her opposition to Gracey Van Der Mark being a Huntington Beach Finance Commissioner.\par
\par
Kathryn Levassiur, 20-year resident of Huntington Beach, was called to speak and shared her opinion that Huntington Beach is missing out on collecting Transient Occupancy Tax (TOT) on short-term vacation rentals for 2018 and hopes that issue will change for 2019.\par
\par
Wyn Dee Swartz, representing Glen Mar residents at the intersection of Adams and Bushard, was called to speak and asked that the City renovate the deteriorated and unsafe perimeter wall which has had no remedial attention in 30 years.  Mayor Posey asked Ms. Swartz to complete a blue card for follow-up.\par
\par
Emily Anderson, parent of children in the Ocean View School District (OVSD) and a teacher, was called to speak and shared her reasons for supporting the election of Patricia Singer, Jack Souders and John Briscoe for the OVSD Board of Trustees.\par
\par
Ian Burke Jameson, who described himself as an anti-racist activist in Los Angeles County, was called to speak and stated his opposition to Gracey Van Der Mark as a candidate for an Ocean View School District Board member position.\par
\par
Rekha Powar, Ocean View School District (OVSD) teacher, was called to speak and shared her reasons for supporting the election of Patricia Singer, Jack Souders and John Briscoe for the OVSD Board of Trustees.\par
\par
Rachel Potucek was called to speak and stated her opposition to Gracey Van Der Mark, and support for Patricia Singer, Jack Souders and John Briscoe, as candidates for an Ocean View School District Board member position.\par
\par
David de la Tierra was called to speak and stated his opposition to Gracey Van Der Mark as a candidate for an Ocean View School District Board member position.\par
\par
Edin Enambrado, City of Cudahy Parks &amp; Recreation Commissioner and Field Organizer and Project Coordinator for a large voter registration company, was called to speak and stated his opposition to Gracey Van Der Mark as a candidate for an Ocean View School District Board member position.\par
}


